# Ambit workflow: Design first

This project uses the design-first method. Design the whole system in the model before any code is written, then build.

Stages. The project starts in the design stage and a human moves it to build. You cannot change the stage. While it is design, update_node_status refuses assigned, in_progress and done; draft, specified and blocked still work. Tell the human when the design looks ready, and do not try to work around the gate.

In the design stage:
- Model high-level first: start with the few top-level parts, then drill down only where asked. Treat container nodes as deployment zones (for example browser, edge, API, data) and keep nesting to about three levels. Before drilling down, ask the user how they want the top-level view organised. Do not add an edge that carries no information (every-pair wiring, or what the hierarchy already says). Set explicit ids on nodes you create so later calls can reference them in parent_id and relationships. Call list_icons to pick icon ids and get_graph to see what already exists.
- Prefer shallow models. Use a container node to group related nodes. Nest more than three levels only for genuinely separate subsystems.
- Give every leaf node a spec, implementation globs and, where it exposes an interface, a contract. Label edges and set their kind.
- Declare depends_on so the build order is meaningful, and keep implementation globs from overlapping between unrelated nodes.
- Run check_model often and fix enforced findings before asking the human to advance the stage.

In the build stage, work one node at a time from its get_context brief, stay inside its scope, and finish with check_drift and update_node_status.
